**Read:** [Chemical Reactor Analysis and Design Fundamentals — Rawlings and Ekerdt](../sources/Chemical-Reactor-Analysis-Rawlings-Ekerdt.lingua#r_7YQAV27Y3J0B537Z6K5HDN8H0A), the complete material and energy balances for continuous stirred/plug-flow and heterogeneous reactors; [Chemical and Biological Reaction Engineering — MIT 10.37](../sources/Reaction-Engineering-MIT-10-37.lingua#r_67AEXD9K1DJ46SV942PAZHZ4BZ), the whole reactor-design and comparison progression; [Inorganic Chemical Technology — Nanda Kishore, NPTEL](../sources/Inorganic-Chemical-Technology-Kishore.lingua#r_77Z8T5RFYFNNXZ7XKZFKPRKDKR), one full continuous industrial-process case.

An ideal stirred reactor uses one uniform outlet/state for its inventory. An ideal plug-flow model tracks material state along a flow coordinate with negligible axial mixing. A packed bed adds catalyst loading, local transport, pressure loss and potentially changing phases. These are models with assumptions, not classifications that guarantee an actual unit behaves ideally.

For a single first-order irreversible constant-density reaction, an isothermal stirred tank has conversion kτ/(1+kτ), while ideal plug flow has 1−e^(−kτ). The comparison changes for reversibility, competing reactions, temperature gradients, catalyst aging, variable density, pressure drop or different product specifications.

Equal nominal residence time does not imply equal heat removal, mixing, useful selectivity or maintenance. A particular reactor arrangement also depends on material compatibility, solids handling, startup/shutdown, feed variability and how product is separated. A numerical conversion advantage can be outweighed by another consequential system duty.

**Work:** derive and independently compare the ideal cases, then reconstruct a complete industrial reactor account from public technical evidence. State where actual flow/transport data support or contradict each idealization. Compare alternatives at the same accepted product output, with uncertainty and full reaction/separation/utility boundaries.

